'use strict';
/* generated by ~/mrs_talk_video/world3d/audio3d.py notes from narration_world3d/scene_NN.md: do not edit */
const NOTES = [
  ["Thank you. I am Simon Vaiva, and this is joint work with the coauthors listed here. The paper came out on the arXiv in July.", "Here is the short version. An infrared obstruction has limited perturbative predictions for heavy hadrons for decades. We remove it. And once it is gone, a whole family of states becomes predictable: these four, and many more."],
  ["Why hadrons made only of heavy quarks? Because they are the cleanest bound states QCD has to offer. Here are the four families we will follow through the talk.", "In all of them the quark mass is far above the momentum, which is far above the binding energy. That hierarchy gives us controlled expansions.", "So they are a precision laboratory, where effective field theory, lattice QCD and experiment can be compared closely.", "And the timing is right. Lattice results for these baryons are now precise, and at the LHC both large experiments see an excess right at the top anti-top threshold.", "Here is the obstacle. In fixed order, the scale dependence is huge, both in binding energies and in the baryon masses. It is as large as the signal we are after."],
  ["First, the framework. We start from QCD at the scale of the heavy-quark mass, and integrate out the hard modes.", "That leaves nonrelativistic QCD. Then we integrate out the soft modes, and what they leave behind are the potentials.", "At the binding-energy scale we arrive at potential N R Q C D. What remains is ordinary quantum mechanics.", "The Hamiltonian is a kinetic term for each quark plus a potential between each pair. These potentials are not modelled: they are matched to QCD, order by order.", "For a quark and an antiquark in a colour singlet, the colour factor is four thirds.", "Now a baryon: a third quark joins, and every pair of quarks gets half of that factor, two thirds. The matching is known to N N L O. At that order three-body forces enter, and we include them at fixed order.", "We work in the static limit. The one over m Q corrections are dropped, and so are the spin effects, which are worth a few percent of the binding."],
  ["Now the problem. Write the perturbative potential as a series in the strong coupling; the full potential is this series plus a constant, lambda R. The coefficients grow factorially with the order, so the series is asymptotic, not convergent. That is the renormalon.", "Here is what that looks like. The terms shrink for a while, reach a minimum, and then blow up. Even if you stop at the smallest term, you are left with an error of order lambda QCD.", "And here is the symptom. A factor of two in the scale moves the triply bottom Omega mass by hundreds of MeV. And in the binding energy at fixed quark mass, the bands jump from order to order: leading order does not even overlap the others.", "The origin is infrared. The series is sensitive to momenta near lambda QCD, and its smallest term sets the best precision it can reach: of order lambda QCD. On the potential, it is this much.", "That uncertainty is a constant: the whole curve can slide up or down by this amount, the same at every distance.", "Its slope cannot change: the force, the derivative of the potential, does not see a constant at all. So only one number is ambiguous, not the shape.", "In the Borel plane, that ambiguity is this branch point, at u equals one half."],
  ["Here is the idea. Rather than living with the factorial growth, we isolate the leading renormalon into an explicit power correction, and resum the growth in closed form.", "The potential becomes three pieces. First, the series we know, order by order: these three terms.", "From each of them we subtract its renormalon part, so the leading factorial growth is gone. The renormalon parts themselves, at every order, go here.", "That is the second piece: the renormalon series, added back at every order. As it stands, it is an infinite sum.", "Summed in the Borel plane, along this path up to the branch point, it turns into this closed form: a known function.", "And third, lambda R. It is a single power correction: one constant, of the size of lambda QCD, the same at every distance. And here, past the branch point, is the part the Borel sum cannot reach.", "That part has exactly the form of lambda R: lambda R takes it in, and comes out free of the leading ambiguity. Lambda R is then absorbed into the quark mass, which we tune until quarkonium matches the spin-averaged one S states. For baryons and tetraquarks it is neglected, because they use the same tuned masses, which already include it. The leading renormalon is isolated in lambda R, and our fit to experiment takes care of lambda R: we never have to deal with it by hand.", "The normalization of the renormalon is well determined: it barely moves from one order to the next. It has converged, so the subtraction rests on a stable number.", "And minimal matters. With the same M S bar inputs, the series is only reorganized, as in the earlier work cited here. It has been used for quark masses of every flavour but top. This is its first use in a bound-state problem."],
  ["Inside a bound-state solver there is a catch. A wavefunction samples every separation. Set the scale to one over r, and at large distance you walk straight out of the perturbative regime, into this red zone.", "The naive choice is this curve, one over r, which falls below lambda QCD at large distance.", "Instead we use a smooth interpolating scale: lambda over r at short distance, saturating at 1 GeV at long distance. No kinks in the force, so the Monte Carlo stays stable.", "The cutoff matters. At 1 GeV the subtraction is active exactly where the bound states live. Push it to 2 GeV and it is rarely used, and the results fall back towards fixed order.", "We vary lambda over a half, one and two, the usual factor-of-two scale variation. The number of active flavours, three, four or five, is fixed by the lightest valence quark."],
  ["We solve the few-body problem with quantum Monte Carlo. First, variational Monte Carlo: minimize the energy over a trial wavefunction. That gives an upper bound and a good starting point.", "Then Green's function Monte Carlo evolves these walkers in imaginary time, which projects onto the ground state. The energy comes down from above and settles on the true ground-state energy.", "The trial wavefunctions depend on the channel, as listed here. For tetraquarks we use Hylleraas forms, the trick first used for helium: the wavefunction is written explicitly in all the pair distances, in both colour arrangements.", "Statistical errors are small for most states, and larger for the tetraquarks and the states with top quarks. The imaginary-time extent and step are tracked as systematics."],
  ["Now the results. This is the binding energy against the quark mass, in fixed order, at the first three orders. The bands are the scale variation. They are broad, and leading order does not even overlap the others: the fingerprint of the renormalon.", "And the same plot with minimal renormalon subtraction. At the bottom mass the bands are two to three times narrower, and the orders now partly overlap. That is what improved convergence looks like.", "We tune the charm and bottom masses to the spin-averaged one S states. From leading to next-to-next-to-leading order, the tuned mass drifts by several percent in fixed order, and hardly at all with M R S.", "And this is where lambda R ends up for these two states: inside the tuned masses.", "The tuned values are these two: effective masses, with lambda R absorbed in them. From here on, everything is a prediction from these two numbers, with the top mass as an input."],
  ["How to read the numbers: statistics first, then the scale variation, with the top mass as an input. The new row up top will hold the states that contain a top quark.", "Toponium first. It lands here, close to what threshold calculations find. Its scale band reaches about three quarters of a GeV on either side, so it is wide, even though the shift from one order to the next is small. This is the zero-width limit, which matters for the LHC excess.", "The charm and bottom masses we just tuned now meet, to build the next meson.", "It is parameter-free: there is nothing left to adjust. The measured B c, the lighter of the two spin states, sits here. Our result is spin-averaged, so we compare it with the spin average, built from that measurement and the lattice B c star: we land about 25 MeV below it.", "These two mesons with a top quark are benchmarks rather than observables: the top decays before it can form them, so there is nothing to compare with."],
  ["Now the triply heavy baryons, where we meet lattice QCD. The first row is ours, at N N L O with renormalon subtraction, each with its statistics and its scale range.", "The second row is lattice QCD. We sit below the lattice, by 125 to 175 MeV.", "Fixed order actually sits closer to the lattice. So the advantage of M R S is not the central value. It is what comes next.", "Here is the scale band for the triply bottom Omega at N N L O. Fixed order, about 700 MeV. With M R S, about 10. Nearly two orders of magnitude, and that is what makes the comparison meaningful.", "And the gap is a pattern, not noise. As a fraction of the mass it falls as the quarks get heavier: a one over the quark mass trend, just what the static limit leaves out.", "The same machinery gives benchmarks with top quarks, all the way up to three top quarks. For the one with three top quarks, the scale band is wide."],
  ["Now a ratio. What these two plots show, the bands and the red lattice points alike, is a triply heavy baryon mass divided by the mass of the quarkonium of the same flavour, where the leading renormalon partly cancels. The dashed line is a lower bound: the baryon cannot be lighter than three halves of the quarkonium.", "With M R S, all three orders collapse into one narrow band, just above the bound. The orders sit on top of each other.", "The lattice points, in red, sit a little higher, and drift down towards our band as the quarks get heavier: the same gap as before, seen again in the ratio.", "A word of caution about fixed order. It looks consistent with the lattice, but only through its enormous bands. Agreement bought with a bigger error bar is not better physics."],
  ["Finally, the tetraquarks. Which ones bind? This ratio compares the tetraquark binding energy with twice that of the mixed-flavour meson: positive means bound, and this dashed line marks zero.", "A positive ratio means bound below the two-meson threshold, and the answer depends on the quark masses.", "Binding needs a hierarchy of masses. Renormalon subtraction tightens the window for binding by about a third compared with fixed order, with the same verdicts. These results stop at next-to-leading order: beyond it, three-body and four-body forces appear, and extending the subtraction to them is left for future work.", "These three bind, with the two top antiquarks compact at the centre in this sketch. These two do not: they try to form, and then fall apart into two mesons.", "The bound ones sit well below threshold, and they stay bound at every value of the scale we tried. For the other two, no binding within our variational space."],
  ["To conclude. This is the first use of minimal renormalon subtraction inside a bound-state calculation, with N N L O static potentials and quantum Monte Carlo.", "So what did renormalon subtraction buy us? The first row: for the triply bottom Omega the scale band is nearly two orders of magnitude narrower.", "And three more diagnostics that move the same way: the shifts between orders, the drift of the tuned masses, and bands that now partly overlap.", "Together, these are the hallmarks of improved convergence. The series is still asymptotic, only the leading renormalon is gone, but every diagnostic says it was the obstruction. And the lambda bands test the scheme; they are not a truncation error.", "The payoff: with shifts this small from order to order, the remaining gap to the lattice becomes interpretable. It is consistent with missing one over m Q physics, not perturbative noise.", "The result is this whole family of predictions, from two tuned masses and the top mass.", "What comes next is on this board, first of all the spin and one over m Q potentials, where the gap points. Thank you, and I am happy to take questions.", "And here is the whole talk at a glance: one place for each part, in the order we went through them."],
  ["The last stop on this map, number fourteen, is the list of key references.", "Here are the key references, and the paper itself is on the arXiv."],
];
